WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.993
This is where the engine stops
accepting Kerberos tickets and NTLM

2
00:00:04.993 --> 00:00:10.025
exchanges and starts accepting
Entra-issued OAuth 2.0 access tokens.

3
00:00:10.025 --> 00:00:16.700
The mechanism is CREATE LOGIN FROM EXTERNAL
PROVIDER, on SQL Server 2022 and later.

4
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.238
The login is a passwordless
external principal in master.

5
00:00:20.238 --> 00:00:24.211
At connection time the client
presents a token whose audience is

6
00:00:24.211 --> 00:00:28.625
database.windows.net, and the engine
validates it and maps it to the login.

7
00:00:28.625 --> 00:00:33.573
CREATE LOGIN looks the principal up
through Microsoft Graph, so the instance's

8
00:00:33.573 --> 00:00:38.825
identity needs Graph read permissions with
admin consent, or login creation fails.

9
00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:45.825
On SQL Server 2025, WITH OBJECT_ID pins
a login when display names aren't unique.

10
00:00:45.825 --> 00:00:48.546
And Entra connections
are always encrypted,

11
00:00:48.546 --> 00:00:51.562
so clients must trust the
instance's certificate.

12
00:00:51.562 --> 00:00:52.600
Three types.

13
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.663
An Entra user login, type E,
named by the user principal name;

14
00:00:56.663 --> 00:01:02.250
UPNs can differ from SAM account names,
so the audit captures that mapping first.

15
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.900
The engine has no MFA logic.

16
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:10.486
Conditional Access enforces MFA, device
compliance and sign-in risk before

17
00:01:10.486 --> 00:01:15.363
the token is issued, with no SQL-level
prompt and no SQL-level bypass.

18
00:01:15.363 --> 00:01:18.863
One limit: FIDO2 security
keys aren't yet supported

19
00:01:18.863 --> 00:01:21.887
for Entra authentication
to SQL Server on Arc.

20
00:01:21.887 --> 00:01:26.613
An Entra group login, type X, is
the familiar AD group pattern.

21
00:01:26.613 --> 00:01:31.238
With a dynamic group driven by HR
attributes, people gain and lose

22
00:01:31.238 --> 00:01:35.950
access as their attributes change,
with no CREATE USER or DROP USER.

23
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.323
That needs a P1 license per member,

24
00:01:38.323 --> 00:01:43.237
and access is only as strong as the
write controls on those attributes.

25
00:01:43.237 --> 00:01:48.852
And an application login, also type E, for
a managed identity or service principal,

26
00:01:48.852 --> 00:01:52.037
replacing a SQL login
and its stored password.

27
00:01:52.037 --> 00:01:55.637
Conditional Access doesn't cover
these; service principals are

28
00:01:55.637 --> 00:01:59.849
reachable only through separately
licensed workload-identity policies.

29
00:01:59.849 --> 00:02:03.742
On an Arc server the managed
identity belongs to the server,

30
00:02:03.742 --> 00:02:07.540
so one login per server is the
honest unit of attribution.
